WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:06.390
Game of Thrones Season 7 Episode 6 begins
with our fellowship of heroes beyond the Wall.

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:10.190
There’s a bit of a culture clash between
Jon and Gendry and Tormund.

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:13.980
Gendry is from the south, while Jon is from
the north.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:19.230
And Tormund, as a wildling, is from the lands
beyond the Wall, north of the north.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:21.859
To him, Jon’s “north” is the south.

00:00:21.859 --> 00:00:23.140
It’s relative.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:27.690
So maybe the white walkers, who come from
the far north “Lands of Always Winter”

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:31.360
, would consider even the wildling lands as
south.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:37.730
Gendry is mad at Beric and Thoros, cause in
Season 3, they sold Gendry to Melisandre,

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:40.370
who used him in a blood magic ritual.

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:45.560
Gendry describes Mel having sex with him,
and the Hound tells him to stop “whinging”.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:51.230
Gendry’s experience with Mel was what you
might call a rape – Gendry was Mel’s prisoner,

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:55.360
and she pressured him into sex that he had
no option to refuse.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:00.870
But the story brushes this off as no big deal,
and so did most of the viewers.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:05.979
Compare that to the rape of Sansa by Ramsay
Bolton, which was similar in that Ramsay had

00:01:05.979 --> 00:01:10.090
power over Sansa, and pressured her into sex
without her consent.

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:15.189
There is a difference in the trauma Sansa
felt here, compared to Gendry’s more relaxed

00:01:15.189 --> 00:01:16.189
response.

00:01:16.189 --> 00:01:21.030
But the fact that Sansa’s scene caused such
widespread media outrage , while Gendry’s

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:28.250
scene was mostly ignored, might say something
about how our society views gender and sex.

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:33.259
Jon and Jorah talk about Jorah’s father
Jeor, who was killed by Night’s Watch mutineers

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:35.030
in Season 3.

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:40.259
Jorah says it’s a horrible way for Jeor
to die, and Jon agrees – which is kinda

00:01:40.259 --> 00:01:45.149
funny cause he died the same way, betrayed
by his brothers at the Watch.

00:01:45.149 --> 00:01:51.049
Jeor gave the Mormont family sword, Longclaw,
to Jon in Season 1 – because Jorah had shamed

00:01:51.049 --> 00:01:53.110
the family by slaving.

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:57.999
Jon now tries to give the sword to Jorah,
but he refuses, and says Jon should keep it.

00:01:57.999 --> 00:02:02.579
It’s a nice gesture, but there are other
Mormonts who might wanna keep their family’s

00:02:02.579 --> 00:02:03.920
most valuable possession.

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:08.090
Jorah’s cousin Lyanna could do some work
with Valyrian steel.

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:12.610
But Jon is the hero and must have his magic
sword.

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:16.890
Down at Winterfell, Arya accuses Sansa of
helping the Lannisters kill their father Ned

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:18.940
– which isn’t true.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:24.170
Sansa repeatedly pleaded for Ned’s life
– to the queen, to the court, and at Ned’s

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:26.440
execution, which Arya saw.

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:31.010
There is this letter supporting the Lannisters
that Sansa wrote, but Cersei made her write

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:36.250
that – Sansa was a child and had no choice
, and Sansa tells Arya this.

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:41.370
But Arya just attacks Sansa for being weak
and stupid , and goes on again about Sansa’s

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:47.340
pretty dresses . So Arya has no real argument
here – she’s just acting out of anger

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:52.640
over her childhood frustrations with “pretty”
Sansa . Her anger’s understandable, but

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:58.740
it’s a huge lack of perspective to be threatening
Sansa for liaising with Lannisters years ago,

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:03.940
when there are actual Lannisters out there
right now who want to kill the Starks – Cersei

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:05.730
is the real enemy.

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:08.340
Has Arya forgotten her plan to kill the queen?

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:14.210
Her criticism of Sansa is also hypocritical
given that Arya served Tywin Lannister in

00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:15.530
Season 2.

00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:19.660
So Arya’s acting really irrationally in
this scene.

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:22.890
Which perfectly plays in to Petyr’s plan.

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:26.370
He planted Sansa’s letter to turn Arya against
her.

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:30.320
And now Sansa is coming to Littlefinger for
help – just as he planned.

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:36.210
He subtly suggests that Sansa, not Jon should
rule the north – which seems to be Petyr’s

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:37.370
overall goal.

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:41.210
He wants Sansa to rule the kingdom, and he
wants to rule Sansa.

00:03:41.210 --> 00:03:44.400
But first, he has to deal with Arya.

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:49.150
Littlefinger suggests that Sansa use Brienne
to deal with Arya somehow – but it’s not

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:51.660
clear what exactly he’s suggesting.

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:54.890
Does he think Brienne would imprison or kill
Arya?

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:56.620
That doesn’t seem plausible.

00:03:56.620 --> 00:04:01.360
Or maybe he wants to send Brienne away so
she can’t protect Arya?

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:05.040
Whatever he’s getting at, Sansa does end
up sending Brienne south to represent her

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:07.210
at the meeting with Cersei.

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:12.130
It makes sense that Sansa needs someone she
trusts at the meeting – but as Brienne points

00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:17.129
out, it’s very risky for Sansa to send away
her most loyal protector at this dangerous

00:04:17.129 --> 00:04:18.129
time.

00:04:18.129 --> 00:04:22.939
Cause in her very next scene, Arya confronts
Sansa again.

00:04:22.939 --> 00:04:25.569
And this time, Arya has an actual argument.

00:04:25.569 --> 00:04:30.199
She suggests that Sansa wants to undermine
Jon and rule the north herself.

00:04:30.199 --> 00:04:34.919
This is what Littlefinger was hinting at,
at Sansa might actually want it.

00:04:34.919 --> 00:04:37.490
She has disagreed with some of Jon’s decisions.

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:40.449
She is proud of her role in the Battle of
the Bastards.

00:04:40.449 --> 00:04:44.199
And she does seem to believe Petyr saying
that she is a good ruler.

00:04:44.199 --> 00:04:50.400
So while Sansa would probably never actually
betray Jon, part of her might want to.

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:53.919
And Arya, with her lying game training, might
see this.

00:04:53.919 --> 00:04:59.389
So it almost makes sense for Arya to pressure
Sansa to deter her from betraying Jon.

00:04:59.389 --> 00:05:04.729
But pointing a knife at her sister and threatening
to kill her and wear her face is… pretty

00:05:04.729 --> 00:05:05.729
extreme.

00:05:05.729 --> 00:05:09.930
Arya’s motivation is again far more personal
than strategic.

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:15.050
Cause Sansa asks about Arya’s collection
of faces, which Arya uses to take on magic

00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:16.050
disguises.

00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:20.699
Arya says that the faces make her feel empowered
to take on whatever identity she wants without

00:05:20.699 --> 00:05:26.460
being forced into gender roles . Which is
not how the Faceless Men are meant to work.

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:30.410
They are a cult who make you give up your
identity and become “no one”, to serve

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:32.449
the god of death.

00:05:32.449 --> 00:05:37.060
But Arya is using their powerful magic for
her own personal gratification.

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:40.490
She’s got great power, but no responsibility.

00:05:40.490 --> 00:05:44.960
So instead of using her magic wisely, she
uses it to threaten her sister for being mean

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:46.749
to her years ago.

00:05:46.749 --> 00:05:50.939
On the one hand, this kind of fits the theme
of Arya rejecting the rules of the Faceless

00:05:50.939 --> 00:05:55.840
Men and choosing instead to wreak revenge
on whoever she doesn’t like at the time.

00:05:55.840 --> 00:06:01.409
But this also goes against Arya’s theme
of returning to her Stark identity, and family,

00:06:01.409 --> 00:06:04.789
which is one of the deepest parts of her arc.

00:06:04.789 --> 00:06:09.090
This whole conflict between Arya and Sansa
feels pretty contrived.

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:11.219
But it may end soon.

00:06:11.219 --> 00:06:16.280
Bran hinted earlier that he knows things about
Petyr’s schemes – if he reveals Littlefinger’s

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:22.259
betrayals, the Stark girls could bond again
over bringing justice to their true enemy.

00:06:22.259 --> 00:06:26.650
Back beyond the Wall, Tormund talks shit with
the Hound, and mentions Brienne.

00:06:26.650 --> 00:06:29.530
Brienne almost killed the Hound in Season
4.

00:06:29.530 --> 00:06:31.590
But Tormund has fallen in love with her.

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:37.599
A relationship between a wildling and a highborn
southern lady could potentially be a big deal.

00:06:37.599 --> 00:06:42.590
In the books, Alys Karstark marries a wildling
leader, which helps solidify the peace between

00:06:42.590 --> 00:06:44.659
wildlings and north.

00:06:44.659 --> 00:06:47.910
Maybe Tormund and Brienne could do something
similar.

00:06:47.910 --> 00:06:53.259
Beric speaks with Jon, and says he doesn’t
look much like his father, so he must look

00:06:53.259 --> 00:06:54.360
his mother.

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:57.279
What Beric is saying is that Jon doesn’t
look like Ned.

00:06:57.279 --> 00:07:02.080
But we know that Jon’s real father is actually
Rhaegar Targaryen – and it’s true that

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:06.130
Jon doesn’t look like him – with his blonde
hair and purple eyes.

00:07:06.130 --> 00:07:08.080
Jon looks more like his mother Lyanna.

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:12.279
There have been so many hints this season
about Jon’s real parents – we’ll surely

00:07:12.279 --> 00:07:15.620
get more on this mystery sometime soon.

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:20.919
The Hound sees a mountain that fits the vision
he saw in Ep 1 – a mountain that looks like

00:07:20.919 --> 00:07:22.419
an arrowhead.

00:07:22.419 --> 00:07:27.129
This same mountain was also visible in Bran’s
vision of the creation of the white walkers.

00:07:27.129 --> 00:07:31.520
Which means that all this is taking place
near the same spot where the Night King was

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:32.520
born.

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:37.589
There’s also symbolism in the Hound looking
at a Mountain, cause his hated brother Gregor

00:07:37.589 --> 00:07:41.719
is called the Mountain – some still hope
the two might fight.

00:07:41.719 --> 00:07:45.699
&lt;Tasteful airhorns&gt;
At Dragonstone, Dany talks to Tyrion about

00:07:45.699 --> 00:07:46.699
heroes.

00:07:46.699 --> 00:07:53.009
She names Drogo, Jorah and Daario, who…
are all brave men, but are they really heroes?

00:07:53.009 --> 00:07:59.370
Drogo was a brutal warlord who attacked innocent
villages to kill, enslave and rape their people.

00:07:59.370 --> 00:08:03.729
Jorah was a slaver who fled justice, and spied
on Daenerys.

00:08:03.729 --> 00:08:08.539
Daario is a mercenary who betrayed his own
comrades, and advises Dany to mass murder

00:08:08.539 --> 00:08:11.939
. By most standards, these guys are no heroes.

00:08:11.939 --> 00:08:15.469
But that’s the thing about Game of Thrones
– some of the good guys are badder than

00:08:15.469 --> 00:08:18.349
the actual bad guys of other shows.

00:08:18.349 --> 00:08:24.099
Dany does name one true hero, though – Jon
Snow – who almost always fights for what’s

00:08:24.099 --> 00:08:25.099
right.

00:08:25.099 --> 00:08:28.819
Heroes like him, Dany says, get themselves
killed – which in Jon’s case is truer

00:08:28.819 --> 00:08:30.639
than she knows.

00:08:30.639 --> 00:08:35.500
They talk about Cersei – who Tyrion expects
will set a trap for Daenerys when they meet

00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:36.500
at King’s Landing.

00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:41.930
So Tyrion says they’ll protect Dany by threatening
to burn the city if she tries anything – but

00:08:41.930 --> 00:08:44.690
they do want to avoid deceit and murder.

00:08:44.690 --> 00:08:49.959
It’s interesting that in previous times
when Dany met enemies, she happily used deceit

00:08:49.959 --> 00:08:54.180
and murder – she betrayed and slayed slavers
and slaves alike at Astapor.

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:58.690
But if Dany wants her rule of Westeros to
last longer than it did in Meereen – she

00:08:58.690 --> 00:09:01.210
needs people to trust her.

00:09:01.210 --> 00:09:06.449
Tyrion raises the question of succession – who
will rule after Dany dies.

00:09:06.449 --> 00:09:11.720
Normally it’d be the ruler’s child, but
Dany has no children, and apparently never

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:12.720
will.

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:17.140
Cause in Book 1, Mirri Maz Duur tells Dany
that she’s infertile . And Dany never got

00:09:17.140 --> 00:09:20.480
pregnant by Daario, so it seems likely true.

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:24.200
So what happens to the realm after Dany dies?

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:28.360
Dany doesn’t wanna talk about it – she
gets prickly when Tyrion talks about her death

00:09:28.360 --> 00:09:33.790
– a bit like a certain author we all know
. But Dany has got to work out something.

00:09:33.790 --> 00:09:38.160
So Tyrion mentions democracy as a possible
way for Westeros to choose their next ruler

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:41.800
– just as the ironborn and the Night’s
Watch vote for their leaders.

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:47.720
This is the first real glimpse we’ve gotten
of what breaking the wheel might mean to Daenerys.

00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:53.089
And it seems she’s serious about changing
politics and society in Westeros.

00:09:53.089 --> 00:09:57.360
Beyond the Wall, Jon’s men are attacked
by a zombie bear, which kills some redshirts

00:09:57.360 --> 00:09:59.300
and savages Thoros.

00:09:59.300 --> 00:10:03.290
Beric sets the bear alight, which doesn’t
kill it for some reason – normally, fire

00:10:03.290 --> 00:10:04.800
kills zombies good.

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:09.180
And the fire makes the Hound too afraid to
attack the bear, cause he’s scared of fire,

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:10.930
cause of his burn.

00:10:10.930 --> 00:10:14.699
But finally Jorah kills the bear with a dragonglass
dagger.

00:10:14.699 --> 00:10:19.459
In the books, dragonglass doesn’t seem to
hurt zombies – it only kills white walkers.

00:10:19.459 --> 00:10:23.560
But in the show, it kills both, so the bear
goes down.

00:10:23.560 --> 00:10:27.850
Thoros seems pretty relaxed about the hole
in his chest – he takes a swig of drink

00:10:27.850 --> 00:10:29.639
and keeps on walking.

00:10:29.639 --> 00:10:32.720
Leaving those poor nameless wildlings in the
snow.

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:37.690
There’s nothing more deadly than having
no dialogue in this show.

00:10:37.690 --> 00:10:42.750
Thoros and Jorah talk about the Siege of Pyke,
a battle ten years ago when Thoros led a charge

00:10:42.750 --> 00:10:47.100
against the ironborn, waving his flaming sword
like “some kind of god”.

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:52.600
But Thoros reveals that he wasn’t brave
– he was just blackout drunk . Which is

00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:57.000
another of those moments in Thrones which
questions the glory of war.

00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:01.180
Eventually the men see a small group of zombies
led by a white walker.

00:11:01.180 --> 00:11:04.250
Maybe these guys are scouting, or hunting
for Benjen.

00:11:04.250 --> 00:11:09.329
Jon’s men attack the dead and when Jon kills
the walker, most of the zombies fall except

00:11:09.329 --> 00:11:10.709
for one.

00:11:10.709 --> 00:11:14.060
Apparently zombies die when you kill the walker
that raised them.

00:11:14.060 --> 00:11:18.029
This one zombie that survived must have been
raised by a different walker.

00:11:18.029 --> 00:11:22.819
This is important information, cause it means
that the whole army of the dead could be destroyed

00:11:22.819 --> 00:11:27.209
if they can just kill the white walkers – which
Jon seems to be getting pretty good at.

00:11:27.209 --> 00:11:31.899
Also, if we assume that the Night King created
the other white walkers – though this baby

00:11:31.899 --> 00:11:36.949
changing process – maybe killing just the
Night King would kill all the other walkers

00:11:36.949 --> 00:11:39.930
as well as the zombies.

00:11:39.930 --> 00:11:45.170
This whole war could end with one swing of
Jon’s sword.

00:11:45.170 --> 00:11:49.460
The problem now, though, is that their captive
zombie screams for help, and the entire army

00:11:49.460 --> 00:11:52.500
of the dead chases Jon’s men.

00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:58.240
Jon chooses this moment to get Gendry to send
a message for help to Daenerys, which is terrible

00:11:58.240 --> 00:11:59.240
timing.

00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:04.190
Jon always knew they were facing danger, why
not ask Dany for help before they’re just

00:12:04.190 --> 00:12:05.430
about to be killed?

00:12:05.430 --> 00:12:10.210
Why not just have Dany come along in the first
place?

00:12:10.210 --> 00:12:15.500
But Jon runs to a convenient island on a convenient
lake which is conveniently just frozen enough

00:12:15.500 --> 00:12:19.319
for Jon to cross but not frozen enough to
let the dead.

00:12:19.319 --> 00:12:24.680
Gendry marathons to Eastwatch, and sends a
raven the thousand miles down to Dragonstone,

00:12:24.680 --> 00:12:28.420
so that Dany will fly the thousand miles up
to save them.

00:12:28.420 --> 00:12:31.180
Which would take days.

00:12:31.180 --> 00:12:36.209
Days of Jon’s men freezing eating and sleeping
on that island.

00:12:36.209 --> 00:12:41.470
And all the while, the Night King and the
dead just stand and watch.

00:12:41.470 --> 00:12:42.950
Which is… weird.

00:12:42.950 --> 00:12:46.699
Even if they can’t cross the water, they
could just throw spears or shoot arrows or

00:12:46.699 --> 00:12:48.560
throw rocks, right?

00:12:48.560 --> 00:12:51.120
But the dead are patient.

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:55.329
They waited about eight thousand years since
the last Long Night, so they maybe they might

00:12:55.329 --> 00:12:58.389
as well wait a few days to for the ice to
freeze.

00:12:58.389 --> 00:13:01.310
And the walkers do seem to enjoy toying with
people.

00:13:01.310 --> 00:13:06.450
In Book 1, they stand in a circle and watch
while a walker fights Waymar Royce , laughing

00:13:06.450 --> 00:13:11.940
and mocking him . So watching Jon freeze and
starve might be their idea of entertainment.

00:13:11.940 --> 00:13:16.950
Thoros dies of his wounds, and the men hold
a little funeral for him.

00:13:16.950 --> 00:13:22.709
In Book 4, Thoros says “it does not matter
how a man begins, but only how he ends”

00:13:22.709 --> 00:13:28.640
. Thoros began this story as a fat drunk failure
of a priest . But he dies as a warrior fighting

00:13:28.640 --> 00:13:30.970
for life against the dead.

00:13:30.970 --> 00:13:34.620
Check out the Thoros video for more on his
character.

00:13:34.620 --> 00:13:38.480
The Hound, like an idiot, throws a rock that
shows the lake is frozen.

00:13:38.480 --> 00:13:41.370
So the dead start to attack Jon’s men.

00:13:41.370 --> 00:13:46.471
Jon uses Valyrian steel, Beric has his flaming
sword, and Tormund and Jorah and Sandor use

00:13:46.471 --> 00:13:48.189
dragonglass weapons.

00:13:48.189 --> 00:13:52.230
So they’ve got exactly the right tools to
counter the dead.

00:13:52.230 --> 00:13:57.300
There are a lot of them though, so shit gets
pretty real before the inevitable Drogon ex

00:13:57.300 --> 00:13:58.389
machina arrives.

00:13:58.389 --> 00:14:02.720
Dany swoops in with her dragons and burns
the dead – a bit like that dream in Book

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:08.089
3 . But as she rescues Jon’s men, the Night
King activates his trap card, and throws an

00:14:08.089 --> 00:14:13.279
ice spear that hits Dany’s dragon Viserion
– killing one of the only three dragons

00:14:13.279 --> 00:14:15.589
alive in Westeros.

00:14:15.589 --> 00:14:20.940
The King has three spears for Dany’s three
dragons – almost as though he knew they

00:14:20.940 --> 00:14:21.940
were coming.

00:14:21.940 --> 00:14:24.579
So some people theorise that this whole thing
was a trap.

00:14:24.579 --> 00:14:29.490
But it’s not clear how the Night King would
know that Dany’s dragons exist – let alone

00:14:29.490 --> 00:14:33.800
that Dany would come save Jon at this time
and place.

00:14:33.800 --> 00:14:36.920
Dany didn’t even know she would come until
a day ago.

00:14:36.920 --> 00:14:42.940
So unless the Night King is straight-up psychic
like Bran, the trap idea doesn’t seem likely.

00:14:42.940 --> 00:14:48.820
After Viserion dies, Jon decides not to immediately
get on Drogon and fly away, but instead to

00:14:48.820 --> 00:14:51.519
kill some more zombies and take a dip in the
lake.

00:14:51.519 --> 00:14:53.560
So Dany leaves without him.

00:14:53.560 --> 00:14:58.399
If Dany had decided instead to attack the
Night King – to burn him, or crash a dragon

00:14:58.399 --> 00:15:02.290
into him – she could’ve ended this whole
war there and then.

00:15:02.290 --> 00:15:03.879
But, she flees.

00:15:03.879 --> 00:15:07.520
And leaves her child Viserion dead in the
lake.

00:15:07.520 --> 00:15:12.009
Jon survives – because at this point falling
into water is practically a guarantee that

00:15:12.009 --> 00:15:13.800
a character will survive.

00:15:13.800 --> 00:15:18.579
Then he faces the army of the dead and prepares
to die – a bit like he did at the Battle

00:15:18.579 --> 00:15:19.620
of the Bastards.

00:15:19.620 --> 00:15:24.339
You’d think that after dying and being reborn,
Jon would’ve learned to stop throwing himself

00:15:24.339 --> 00:15:29.370
into impossibly dangerous situations, but
it seems he’s decided he can take as many

00:15:29.370 --> 00:15:32.360
risks as he likes and he’ll always survive.

00:15:32.360 --> 00:15:39.449
Cause he always somehow does – Benjen saves
him at the last minute and Jon gets away again.

00:15:39.449 --> 00:15:44.221
One of the moments that defined Game of Thrones
Season 1 was the death of Ned Stark, which

00:15:44.221 --> 00:15:47.999
showed that when good guys make bad mistakes,
they die.

00:15:47.999 --> 00:15:51.769
In Season 3, Robb and Catelyn made mistakes
and they died.

00:15:51.769 --> 00:15:58.470
But by rescuing Jon from certain death over
and over and over, saving Jaime, saving Arya

00:15:58.470 --> 00:16:01.410
– it takes away the tension and drama.

00:16:01.410 --> 00:16:05.899
Every time this happens, we care less.

00:16:05.899 --> 00:16:10.939
Jon’s reunion with Benjen is one of the
most long-awaited moments in the series.

00:16:10.939 --> 00:16:16.740
For years – five books and seven seasons
– Jon has been hoping to see his uncle again.

00:16:16.740 --> 00:16:21.259
And in the books there are hints that Benjen
knows secrets about Jon’s birth – he was

00:16:21.259 --> 00:16:25.170
involved in the Knight of the Laughing Tree
incident which may have led to Jon’s parents

00:16:25.170 --> 00:16:27.250
hooking up – go watch that video.

00:16:27.250 --> 00:16:30.589
But in the show, there’s no time for that
detail.

00:16:30.589 --> 00:16:34.509
Benjen turns up, says ‘Hi, I’m a zombie,
goodbye”, and decides he’d rather die

00:16:34.509 --> 00:16:36.970
than share a horse with his nephew.

00:16:36.970 --> 00:16:42.089
It does feel a bit anticlimactic, but there
is pathos implied in Ben’s death.

00:16:42.089 --> 00:16:47.800
Benjen has apparently been surviving in the
north on his own as a zombie man for years.

00:16:47.800 --> 00:16:51.009
He’s probably suffered in ways that we can’t
imagine.

00:16:51.009 --> 00:16:56.230
But he kept fighting all this time until he
got the chance to do something noble, saving

00:16:56.230 --> 00:17:02.399
Jon, before letting go – he was ready to
die, and ended it on his terms.

00:17:02.399 --> 00:17:06.779
So the reunion’s a bit janky, but there
is meaning here.

00:17:06.779 --> 00:17:09.339
The Hound carries the captive zombie through
the Wall.

00:17:09.339 --> 00:17:18.809
The way this seems to work is that the dead
can pass through the Wall only when the living

00:17:18.809 --> 00:17:21.909
carry them through – like with Othor and
Jafer Flowers in Season 1.

00:17:21.909 --> 00:17:23.750
But the magic of the Wall stops the dead getting
through uninvited . That being the case, you’d

00:17:23.750 --> 00:17:28.040
think that Benjen could have crossed the Wall
if someone just carried him – but he’s

00:17:28.040 --> 00:17:31.260
not an ordinary zombie, so he might work differently.

00:17:31.260 --> 00:17:35.669
Either way, the heroes have succeeded in their
insane plot to catch a zombie.

00:17:35.669 --> 00:17:38.480
Hopefully it’ll be worth it.

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:43.330
Dany stands on the Wall and waits for Jon,
or maybe she’s hoping for Viserion.

00:17:43.330 --> 00:17:49.000
The dragon doesn’t come, but Jon does – collapsed
on his horse just like he was in Season 3.

00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:53.330
Dany sees his chest and learns that he really
was stabbed in the heart, and that he’s

00:17:53.330 --> 00:17:55.510
been keeping up with his situps.

00:17:55.510 --> 00:17:59.720
When he wakes, Jon says he’s sorry about
the death of her dragon – Dany did say that

00:17:59.720 --> 00:18:02.440
her dragons are the only children she’ll
ever have.

00:18:02.440 --> 00:18:08.240
But some people speculate that the death of
Viserion might allow Dany to have kids again.

00:18:08.240 --> 00:18:12.659
Because Dany’s infertility seems to be a
sort of a curse from Mirri Maz Duur, who once

00:18:12.659 --> 00:18:15.870
said that only death can pay for life.

00:18:15.870 --> 00:18:20.320
Maybe the death of her dragon will allow Dany
to make life again.

00:18:20.320 --> 00:18:25.620
Dany and Jon having kids might well be on
the cards, from all the talk of kids and succession

00:18:25.620 --> 00:18:30.279
this episode, and from the way Jon and Dany
are looking at each other lately.

00:18:30.279 --> 00:18:34.890
Even though they’re aunt and nephew – in
fact due to generations of royal in-breeding,

00:18:34.890 --> 00:18:38.740
this is, like, super-incest.

00:18:38.740 --> 00:18:43.120
But regardless, Jon says he’ll kneel to
Dany and make her his queen, finally forming

00:18:43.120 --> 00:18:46.350
a great alliance to face the dead.

00:18:46.350 --> 00:18:52.380
Meanwhile, the dead pull Viserion out of the
lake, like ants pulling a worm.

00:18:52.380 --> 00:18:55.910
Some people have questioned how the dead got
these chains – but the walkers have been

00:18:55.910 --> 00:19:00.000
around for thousands of years, it’s plausible
enough that they found some chains from a

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:03.400
ship or a merchant or that scythe at some
point.

00:19:03.400 --> 00:19:08.020
So they pull out the dragon, and the Night
King raises it as a zombie – a Blue Eyes

00:19:08.020 --> 00:19:09.040
Wight Dragon.

00:19:09.040 --> 00:19:13.700
Since the Night King turns the dragon by touching
it, it seems possible that this dragon is

00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:19.000
now more like a white walker than just a regular
zombie – like how the Night King changed

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:20.000
that baby.

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:23.309
The baby was alive though, while Viserion’s
dead.

00:19:23.309 --> 00:19:28.669
So there’s lots of uncertainty about what
the zombie dragon’s powers will be.

00:19:28.669 --> 00:19:30.320
Will it breathe fire?

00:19:30.320 --> 00:19:33.090
Seems unlikely, when fire kills zombies.

00:19:33.090 --> 00:19:37.710
Maybe it’ll breathe frost, like an ice dragon,
which is a whole other thing, a species of

00:19:37.710 --> 00:19:39.680
dragon that’s made out of ice.

00:19:39.680 --> 00:19:42.900
Whatever the specifics, zombie dragons are
really bad news.

00:19:42.900 --> 00:19:46.760
This is NK getting nukes, and they will use
it.

00:19:46.760 --> 00:19:53.610
No one is safe, and Dany and Jon must bring
fire and fury to stop them.

00:19:53.610 --> 00:19:58.539
In this episode, Beric says that the enemy
is death, and “Maybe we don’t need to

00:19:58.539 --> 00:20:00.710
understand any more than that”.

00:20:00.710 --> 00:20:05.740
It’s like Beric is telling the audience
we don’t need to understand how Jon survived

00:20:05.740 --> 00:20:09.830
on that island for days, or why the Night
King stood and watched.

00:20:09.830 --> 00:20:13.390
We don’t need to understand why Arya suddenly
turned on her sister.

00:20:13.390 --> 00:20:16.220
Or how Jon survives again and again.

00:20:16.220 --> 00:20:19.420
Or how Cersei’s reign works politically.

00:20:19.420 --> 00:20:21.500
And maybe we don’t need to understand.

00:20:21.500 --> 00:20:25.630
But Game of Thrones at its best isn’t just
about battles and dragons.

00:20:25.630 --> 00:20:30.310
It’s complex politics and human drama that
makes you think.

00:20:30.310 --> 00:20:36.510
David and Dan do an amazing job of adapting
a story that George Martin wrote to be unadaptable

00:20:36.510 --> 00:20:40.659
– and at this point they’ve even run out
of books to adapt.

00:20:40.659 --> 00:20:46.460
But hopefully these last seasons of Thrones
can conclude the story without losing that

00:20:46.460 --> 00:20:51.350
layer of detail and depth that makes Thrones
unique.

00:20:51.350 --> 00:20:52.740
Thanks for watching.

00:20:52.740 --> 00:20:58.240
Thanks to Patrons Jesse Deal, Charlie Fox,
Hannah Hurst, Nicola Thompson, Bruno Zorzi,

00:20:58.240 --> 00:21:01.039
Shelbelle and Jpop Olopolous.

00:21:01.039 --> 00:21:01.759
Cheers.

